# Commit Rules (Strict)

When generating commits:

- **MUST** use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) format
- **MUST NOT** generate generic messages
- **MUST** scope commits to domain areas: `cube`, `solver`, `rendering`, `ui`

If unsure:

- Choose the closest module scope
- Prefer smaller commits over large mixed changes

```
# ❌ BAD — generic, unscoped
update files
fix stuff
wip

# ✅ GOOD — conventional + scoped + specific
feat(cube): add scramble validity check
fix(solver): correct beginner layer-2 edge orientation
test(cube): add inverse move invariant tests
refactor(rendering): extract sticker interpolation helper
```

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>

[optional body — why, not what]
```

**Types:** `feat`, `fix`, `test`, `refactor`, `chore`, `docs`  
**Scopes:** `cube`, `solver`, `rendering`, `ui` (pick one per commit)

## Core principle

One logical change per commit. If changes span modules, split into separate scoped commits.

## Agent commits

- **MUST NOT** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor` or any Cursor co-author trailer
- When the agent creates commits, use `git commit-tree` (not `git commit`) so the message stays exactly as written
